{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Arur</b>. For the serpent, that its life be short. And the correct meaning is that it shall be lacking in its walking." "“Upon your belly (gikhonkha)”: the chest. And we do not know whether the nun is a root letter, as in אדון, or added, as in זדון. And one who says it is so called because of the רוח (spirit/breeze) that emerges (יגיח) from it — this is the way of derash. So too they said of Gihon; likewise Pishon from the root of כי תפושו (Jeremiah 50:11); likewise Perat from the root of פרו ורבו. Now the great river is the Tigris; it stood before them and they could not cross, and they said: it is two words — the waters are חדים (sharp) and קלים (light)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {1 "The passage transitions abruptly from etymological discussion of river names to an apparent anecdote about the Tigris; translated hyper-literally as the text stands without resolving the disjoint."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:43:02.392309111Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}